          content: [
            `The eastern highway leading out of Adious City stretched into tranquil silence.`,
            `Once past the glittering glass towers of the capital, the clamor of traffic faded away until only the whisper of the wind and the rustle of autumn leaves remained. Out here, the eastern countryside opened into wide, gentle expanses—golden harvest fields, scattered farmsteads, and distant mountain peaks half-veiled in evening mist. The crisp air smelled of rich soil and ripened grain; at long last, the outskirts were at peace.`,
            `Out at the Reina Farm Estate, dozens of children ran freely across the sprawling green pastures, their bright laughter ringing out across the valley as the setting sun painted the sky in warm shades of amber and rose.`,
            `Over by the timber barn, Andy slid the heavy wooden doors shut and latched the iron bolt, the golden light of dusk catching the rugged lines of his face as he called out across the yard.`,
            `"Alright, you rascals, that's enough playing for today! Go wash your hands and faces—it's almost time for Thanksgiving dinner!"`,
            `The children let out a chorus of playful groans but quickly obeyed, gathering up their toys and balls before racing one another toward the warm lights of the main farmhouse.`,
            `Inside, the spacious country kitchen glowed with the warm light of oil lamps and the hearth fire. With her sleeves rolled up to her elbows, Merin stood beside Andy's mother, Mrs. Velt, stirring a large pot of vegetable soup while several of the older youths busily arranged plates and silverware along the dining tables.`,
            `Watching the lively bustle around them, Mrs. Velt smiled fondly, the deep lines around her eyes softening. "My, what a hectic day... Still, it's a wonderful kind of busy, isn't it, dear? So peaceful, after everything we've had to endure."`,
            `Pausing her ladle, Merin glanced around the warm room—taking in the savory aroma of roasted chicken, the cheerful chatter of the children, and the small hands setting out cups. A quiet, heartfelt smile touched her lips.`,
            `"Yes... Honestly, I feel blessed just to be alive to see a day like this," Merin said softly. "Just to hear them all laughing again."`,
            `Her gaze drifted toward the kitchen window, where the last golden rays of sunset brushed across the rolling pastures outside.`,
            `"Mother Reina left this farm behind for us—a safe haven tucked away from the world, full of warmth and peace."`,
            `Looking over at the nurses and caretakers who had remained steadfastly loyal to Reina's memory through the darkest hours, Merin felt the heavy knot in her chest loosen at last. Yet beneath her quiet gratitude, her thoughts drifted toward two other souls resting not far away—her biological mother and father.`,
            `Turning gently toward Mrs. Velt, Merin untied her apron. "Please sit down and rest your legs now, ma'am. Tell the nurses to go ahead and start serving dinner on time—I have somewhere I need to visit for a little while."`,
            `Understanding immediately, Mrs. Velt patted her arm warmly and walked her to the front porch, waving as Merin stepped out into the golden twilight. Leaving the farmstead behind, Merin walked down the quiet dirt lane winding toward the small hillside cemetery at the end of the road, while far to the West, the blazing sun dipped slowly behind the distant silhouette of Adious City, sending a cool evening breeze rippling across the vast grasslands.`,
            `* * *`,
            `By the time Merin reached the quiet cemetery nestled at the foot of the hill, the western sky burned a deep, luminous crimson.`,
            `Side by side beneath a swaying willow tree stood two weathered stone graves. Dropping to her knees in the soft grass, Merin gently brushed the autumn dust from the engraved names of her mother and father and cleared away the stray weeds around the base. Then, with fingers trembling slightly, she laid two fresh bouquets of wildflowers before the headstones.`,
            `"Mom... Dad... Happy Thanksgiving," Merin whispered, her voice catching in her throat. "I've grown up now... and I miss you both so, so much."`,
            `The cool evening wind brushed through her light brown hair, drying the tears on her lashes before they could fall. For a fleeting heartbeat, all her armor melted away, leaving her feeling like that frightened little girl of three again—small, lost, and reaching out for a warmth she could never touch. Pressing her forehead against the cool granite, she squeezed her eyes shut, fighting back her sobs.`,
            `Just then, up on the paved ridge road overlooking the cemetery, the quiet thud of a car door closing echoed on the breeze, followed by two faint voices drifting down the slope.`,
            `"Would you like me to come down with you, Enri?" Olivie asked softly.`,
            `"No, stay warm in the car," Enri answered quietly. "I'll be back in just a few minutes."`,
            `Measured footsteps rustled through the tall autumn grass down the hillside, the hem of Enri's dark trench coat fluttering against the fading crimson light. Hearing someone approaching behind her, Merin quickly wiped her eyes with her sleeve and rose to her feet, turning around in surprise.`,
            `"Is... is that you, Enri?"`,
            `Stopping a few paces away, Enri looked at her with quiet, uncertain eyes. "Yeah... It's me."`,
            `Walking slowly up beside the graves, Enri knelt on one knee, set down a woven basket of pure white lilies before their parents' headstones, and bowed his head in silent reverence.`,
            `For a long while, neither sibling spoke a word. All around them, the world was hushed and still, save for the whisper of the wind through the grass and the faint, distant hum of the capital miles away on the horizon.`,
            `Finally, Enri broke the silence, his voice low and heavy with remorse. "Merin... I'm sorry. For everything I did... and for all the years I wasn't there."`,
            `Merin shook her head gently, her eyes following a flock of birds rising into the amber twilight. "None of it was your fault, Enri. Maybe all of it was just... cruel fate."`,
            `Enri hesitated, his hands clenching slightly at his sides as he looked at her. "Fate, maybe... Tell me honestly, Merin—after everything that's happened between us, can we still—"`,
            `Before he could even finish the question, Merin stepped forward and threw her arms tightly around his neck.`,
            `"Family isn't something decided by fate," Merin whispered fiercely against his shoulder. "We're brother and sister forever, Enri."`,
            `For a long moment, the two of them simply stood there holding on to one another—surrounded by the whispering wind, the silent stones, and the last golden light of day.`,
            `Slowly, Enri raised his hand and rested it gently atop her head, the exact same way he used to comfort her when they were small children. Behind his eyes, the warmest memories of their childhood shone bright and clear—flying a torn paper kite across the meadow, their father's warm laughter, and their mother's gentle voice calling them home for supper.`,
            `On the horizon, the crimson sun slipped completely behind the skyline of Adious, turning the twilight sky from burnished gold to tranquil indigo.`,
            `"Everything is going to be alright from here on out," Enri murmured softly, resting his chin atop her hair. "No matter how much we lost... you and I are still family."`,
            `And in that sacred stillness, the evening wind carried their vow out across the open plains—soft, fragile, and eternal.`
          ],
